==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ǵʰelh₃-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|colerik}}, from {{uder|en|fro|colerique}}, from {{uder|en|la|cholericus}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkɒl(ə)ɹɪk/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-choleric.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkɑlɝɪk/|/kəˈlɛɹɪk/|a=GA}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkɔl(ɘ)ɹɘk/|/kɘˈliəɹɘk/|a=NZ}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# (''according to theories of the four [[w:humorism|humours]] or [[w:Four temperaments|temperaments]]'') Having a [[temperament]] characterized by an excess of [[choler]]; easily becoming [[angry]].
#* {{RQ:Bacon Essayes|Of Travel|text=And let a man beware, how he keepeth company with '''choleric''' and quarrelsome persons; for they will engage him into their own quarrels.}}
#* '''1640''', {{w|George Herbert}}, ''Jacula Prudentum; or, Outlandish Proverbs, Sentences, etc.'', in ''The Remains of that Sweet Singer of the Temple George Herbert'', London: Pickering, 1841, p. 146,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/remainsofthats00her]</sup>
#*: From a '''choleric''' man withdraw a little; from him that says nothing for ever.
#* {{RQ:Dickens Old Curiosity Shop|35|text=As it was clear that he was a '''choleric''' fellow in some respects, Mr Swiveller was relieved to find him in such good humour, and, to encourage him in it, smiled himself.}}
#* {{RQ:Mitchell Gone with the Wind|passage=Beneath his '''choleric''' exterior Gerald O’Hara had the tenderest of hearts.}}
# Showing or expressing [[anger]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-2|I|2|text=What, what, my lord! are you so '''choleric'''<br>With Eleanor, for telling but her dream?}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1667|author=w:John Dryden|title=w:Sir Martin Mar-all|section=act V, scene 1|url=https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/006104554
|passage=How angry the poor devil is! In fine, thou art as '''choleric''' as a cook by a fireside.}}
#* {{RQ:Arbuthnot Law|part=2|chapter=An Account of the Conference between Mrs. Bull and Don Diego Dismallo|page=20|passage=For God’s ſake, Madam, vvhy ſo '''Cholerick'''? I ſay, this Letter is ſome Forgery; {{...}}}}
# Of or relating to [[cholera]] {{gl|infectious disease}}.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1833|author=w:David Craigie|title=Observations, pathological and therapeutic, on the epidemic cholera, as it has prevailed in Edinburgh and its vicinity|journal=Edinburgh Medical and Surgical Journal|issue=114|titleurl=https://archive.org/details/b21948306|page=21
|passage=I now proceed to enumerate those lesions and morbid changes found in the bodies of those cut off by cholera, which must have preceded the appearance of the disease, and which, existing, as they did, in very different organs, had no connection with the '''choleric''' symptoms, but nevertheless rendered the chance of recovery much less likely.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Causing an excess of [[choler]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Taming of the Shrew|IV|3|text=I fear it is too '''choleric''' a meat.<br>How say you to a fat tripe finely broil’d?}}

